This is a geometry problem.  We can use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the length of the diameter.  The Pythagorean Theorem states that the square of the hypotenuse of a right triangle is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two sides.  In this case, the hypotenuse is the diameter of the circle, and the other two sides are the distances from the center of the circle to the endpoints of the diameter.  We can find these distances by subtracting the coordinates of the center of the circle from the coordinates of the endpoints of the diameter.  For example, the distance from the center of the circle to the endpoint at $(-1,-1)$ is $|-1-2|=3$.  The distance from the center of the circle to the endpoint at $(-1,-1)$ is $|-1-3|=4$.  Therefore, the length of the diameter is $\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=\sqrt{25}=5$.  Since the diameter is a line segment, it has two endpoints.  The other endpoint is the point that is 5 units away from the center of the circle in the opposite direction.  The coordinates of this point are $(2-5,3-5)=(-3,-2)$.  Therefore, the coordinates of the other endpoint of the diameter are $(-3,-2)$.
&lt;&lt;&lt;answer&gt;&gt;&gt;
The coordinates of the other endpoint of the diameter are $(-3,-2)$.

